 complication of the administration of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochlorothiazide"},{"label":"B","text":"bumetanide"},{"label":"C","text":"furosemide"},{"label":"D","text":"acetazolamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0470380154c8","stem":"which methyldopa is most contraindicated is","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cfa2a88a7657","stem":"which hydralazine is most contraindicated is","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f48631a1705a","stem":"which reserpine is most contraindicated is","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca7d0e466979","stem":"which spironolactone is most contraindicated is","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8e2fe5c6aea4","stem":"A patient is diagnosed as suffering from herpes simples genitals. Which drug would you prescribe to the patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amantadine"},{"label":"B","text":"Ritonavir"},{"label":"C","text":"Pentamidine"},{"label":"D","text":"Acyclovir"},{"label":"E","text":"Forscanet"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a3ba8c3636f84ed5dc8407431926980723e3b680"},{"id":"q-a1a2070f1f0c","stem":"Patients who are slow in activators of isoniazid are likely to develop one of the following adverse effects?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"renal insufficiency"},{"label":"B","text":"vestibular damage"},{"label":"C","text":"peripheral neuritis"},{"label":"D","text":"optic neuritis"},{"label":"E","text":"color blindness"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c76c57171f60","stem":"Ganciclovir .........T...Use is limited to life or site threatening CMV infection in immunocompromised patients .........F...... Is effective only against Influenza A .........F...... Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor .........T...... Competes with guanosine triphosphate for incorporation into viral DNA .........T...... Concomitant use of potential bone marrow depressant drugs should be avoided 13 Which of the following induces microsomal enzymes?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b4ec54bde63d","stem":"Which DNA polymerase inhibitor is used only for life or sight threatening Cytomegalovirus infection in patient who is immunocompromised? And why? (4marks)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ganciclovir - Phosypholirated nucleotide that inhibit DNA polymerase"},{"label":"B","text":"Reason it delay HIV related complication including kaposis sarcoma in imucompromised patient and it delay CMV retinitis in HIV"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e64f2e62c78f558fe112f78d2e7f63ac8307955b"},{"id":"q-e17a55cd9043","stem":"Which antiretroviral combination drugs are used for Individuals with Tuberculosis co- infection?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-97673ec92c82","stem":"When M3 receptors are activated by acetylcholine the membrane potential of a smooth muscle becomes less negative, that is the muscle is more excitable. This explains the increase in tonic tension and in the number of contractions brought about by acetylcholine. By blocking M3 receptors antimuscarinic drugs cause the opposite, that is the resting membrane potential becomes more negative and the muscle is less excitable. The postreceptor mechanism triggered by activation of M3 receptors is the stimulation of phospholipase C which in turn increases the synthesis of IP3. By blocking M3 receptors the opposite will occur.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd888afd8e1e","stem":"When complicating inferior myocardial infarction, complete AV block often results from an increased parasympathetic tone and usually respond to atropine. Sometimes the block resolve spontaneously, but in the present case the block must be treated since the heart rate is too low for a sufficient cardiac output.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Parasympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06a9f8896ff6","stem":"Previous Classification of Diabetes Mellitus: Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (IDDM) = Type 1 Non-Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus (NIDDM) = Type 2 2 Classification ... • In 1997, The American Diabetes Association adopted the following guidelines for the classification of diabetes types, based on aetiology. • Type 1 - results from an autoimmune response to pancreatic ß-cell component(s) triggered by viral infection • Type 2 - hyperglycemia may be due to: the external secretions to keep dogs with diabetes alive?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased hepatic glucose production"},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired insulin secretion"},{"label":"C","text":"Receptor and post receptor defects (insulin resistance) • Chronic hyperglycemia of diabetes is associated with long-term damage, dysfunction, and failure of various organs, especially the eyes, kidneys, nerves, heart and blood vessels. 3 Diabetes – Insulin (Biosynthesis, storage, secretion) Insulin gene is on chromosome 11 Synthesized largely by β cells of the Islets of Langerhans (pancreas) as a 12,000 Dalton precursor (preproinsulin) The insulin mRNA is translated as a single chain precursor called preproinsulin Processed to final secreted products (proinsulin, insulin, C-peptide). Removal of signal peptide during insertion into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) generates proinsulin Synthesis is regulated at the transcriptional and translational levels. 4 Proinsulin • Proinsulin consists of 3 domains: an amino-terminal B chain, a carboxy-terminal A chain and a connecting peptide in the middle known as the C peptide. • Within the ER, proinsulin is exposed to several specific endopeptidases which excise the C peptide, generating the mature form of insulin. • Insulin and free C peptide are packaged in the Golgi into secretory granules which accumulate in the cytoplasm. • When the beta cell is appropriately stimulated, insulin is secreted from the cell by exocytosis and diffuses into islet capillary blood. C peptide is also secreted into blood, but has no known biological activity. 5 Proinsulin to Insulin Within the ER, proinsulin is exposed to several specific endopeptidases which excise the C peptide, thereby generating the mature form of insulin Stored as β granules Exists as a monomer, dimer or hexamer; Each hexamer binds two molecules of Zn2+ which coordinates crystal formation within β granules of insulin 6 Insulin Consist of A & B chains linked by 2 disulfide bonds (plus additional disulfide in A) Positions B24 and B25 are important sites for receptor recognition; Substitution at these sites – a product with a marked change in biological activity. ~ A = 21amino acids B = 30 amino acids 7 Banting and Best 8 The birth of an idea In Ontario, Canada, first conceived the idea - discovery of insulin. One evening, after delivering a lecture on the pancreas to medical students, he was struck by an idea: Could the internal secretions of the pancreas be isolated from - tie-off pancreatic ducts to cause acinar tissue degeneration, thereby removing proteases which were destroying the anti- diabetic principle during its extraction. 9 The Discovery of insulin Banting began his research on May 19, 1921, with Macleod as formal supervisor and Charles Banting as his assistant. In August 1921 after numerous failures, Banting and Best prepared a new extract from the atrophied pancreas of one of the dogs. They then isolated two other dogs with diabetes, administering the extract to one and leaving the second untreated. Four days later, the untreated dog died of severe diabetes. The dog that received the extract lived for 3 more weeks, dying only when the extract was used up - Marjorie. 10 Marjorie 11 First Human Patient On Jan. 11, 1922, 14-year-old Leonard Thompson was the first human patient to receive insulin made by Banting and Best. The initial test failed, causing only slight reductions in blood glucose levels. A second series of \"purified\" insulin injections, produced by J.B. Collip, achieved the desired results. Leonard's blood glucose dropped to normal, and he began to gain weight. 12 Insulin therapy Prior to 1972, purity of commercial insulin preparations was low and the insulin contained 5-10% impurities, proinsulin being the major contaminant. Insulin preparations are now recombinant and lack proinsulin and other contaminants. The clinical significance of purified insulin preparations has been a reduction in side effects thought to be caused by immunological issues including:"},{"label":"A","text":"lipodistrophy"},{"label":"B","text":"insulin allergy"},{"label":"C","text":"antibody-related insulin resistance"},{"label":"D","text":"prolonged circulation of injected insulin may contribute to hypoglycemia"},{"label":"E","text":"immune complex deposition 13 Factors Affecting Insulin Secretion"},{"label":"A","text":"Stimulatory Factors Metabolic components glucose* / amino acids / fatty acids / ketones Hormonal components – incr cAMP – incr Ca2+ Cholinergic and β2-adrenergic stimulation (propranolol decrease) Intestinal nutrients via gastrin, secretin, enteroglucagon - oral vs. iv glucose yields a larger response"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibitory Factors - Decrease cAMP Levels insulin / epinephrine / adrenergic stimulation / serotonin 14 Mechanism of Insulin Action 1. Insulin binds to an α subunit of the Insulin Receptor activating the tyrosine kinase domain on its β subunits - Receptor autophosphorylation leads to activation of cellular effectors and a biologic response • A key response is recruitment of glucose transporters to the cell surface in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue  Oral insulin-mimetic has been described 15 Insulin action ... Facilitated diffusion along a downhill gradient is assured by the phosphorylation of glucose to G-6-P."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"c834f65e95127abe09386bfdb3d6e5620710fc2a"},{"id":"q-94889c6cabd0","stem":"Which of the following drugs has an average half life of 50 hours?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nortriptylline"},{"label":"B","text":"Digoxin"},{"label":"C","text":"Trimethoprim"},{"label":"D","text":"Valproic acid"},{"label":"E","text":"Lithium"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-869b10d77a5d","stem":"All of the following have 100% oral bioavailability EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valproic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Trimethoprim"},{"label":"C","text":"Digoxin"},{"label":"D","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"E","text":"Lithium"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5369dcad510","stem":"All of the following drugs are >90% plasma protein bound EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"B","text":"Frusemide"},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoxetine"},{"label":"D","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Warfarin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-85cc2d86a9f6","stem":"Which of the following drugs has a volume of distribution >2000 l/70kg?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin"},{"label":"B","text":"Imipramine"},{"label":"C","text":"Digoxin"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Chloroquine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-102b7383d164","stem":"What is the half life of a drug with a volume of distribution of 100l/70kg and a clearance of 7l/hr/70kg","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 hours"},{"label":"B","text":"10 hours"},{"label":"C","text":"12.5 hours"},{"label":"D","text":"15 hours"},{"label":"E","text":"20 hours"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-b3c0c9c9636d","stem":"All of the following drugs exhibit flow dependent elimination EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atenolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"C","text":"Propoxyphene"},{"label":"D","text":"Amitriptylline"},{"label":"E","text":"Lignocaine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb4106edaefe","stem":"Which of the following undergoes a phase I hydrolysis reaction? (biotransformation)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethanol"},{"label":"B","text":"Naloxone"},{"label":"C","text":"Morphine"},{"label":"D","text":"Lignocaine"},{"label":"E","text":"Diazepam"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-482690e28775","stem":"Which of the following undergoes acetylation in the liver? (Phase 2 biotransformation) Which of these drugs is the most efficacious?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaminophen"},{"label":"C","text":"Salicylic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Diazepam 9. A D B C Concentration"},{"label":"A","text":"Drugs A and B"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug C"},{"label":"C","text":"Drugs A and D"},{"label":"D","text":"Drugs D and C"},{"label":"E","text":"Drug A"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f3dd144f671d","stem":"Referring to the graph in question 9 – which of the drugs is most potent?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"A and B"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fece23ddf0dc","stem":"Which of the following will NOT alter the volume of distribution of a drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac failure"},{"label":"B","text":"Clearance"},{"label":"C","text":"Age"},{"label":"D","text":"Burns"},{"label":"E","text":"Pleural effusion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d65eb74ce42f","stem":"Volume of distribution equals","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dose given/plasma concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"Total amount of drug in the body/plasma concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"Urine drug concentration/plasma concentration"},{"label":"D","text":"Dose given/urine concentration"},{"label":"E","text":"Urine drug concentration/plasma concentration"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-723903611f85","stem":"Which of the following drugs undergoes rate limited elimination?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lignocaine"},{"label":"B","text":"Morphine"},{"label":"C","text":"Warfarin"},{"label":"D","text":"Propanolol"},{"label":"E","text":"Aspirin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4fbdc79b75d4","stem":"Ligand gated channel receptors include all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"GABA"},{"label":"B","text":"Aspartate"},{"label":"C","text":"Glycine"},{"label":"D","text":"Glutamate"},{"label":"E","text":"Ach-muscarinic"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c8d03ff7aa75","stem":"Regarding receptor regulation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Receptor down regulation occurs over hours – days"},{"label":"B","text":"Receptor responses to drugs often “desensitise” with time – this desensitisation is usually irreversible"},{"label":"C","text":"The mechanism of agonist induced desensitisation of the nicotinic Ach receptor has been worked out in detail"},{"label":"D","text":"All “internalised” receptors are degraded by lysosomes"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above are correct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bc779256f032","stem":"Which of the following acts on intracellular receptors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serotonin"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucagon"},{"label":"C","text":"Corticosteroids"},{"label":"D","text":"GABA"},{"label":"E","text":"Insulin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-629cf8d33f53","stem":"Which of the following has ( bioavailability in the neonate when compared with older children/adults?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Digoxin"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetaminophen"},{"label":"D","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenobarbital"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e66d3914520f","stem":"First order kinetics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Means rate of reaction is proportional to concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"Are more common than zero order kinetics"},{"label":"C","text":"Apply to exponential processes"},{"label":"D","text":"Generally apply to high plasma concentrations (>20mg/100ml) of ethanol"},{"label":"E","text":"Result in steady state concentrations after multiple dosing"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c5c0e8058ef","stem":"A single compartment model means that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"One exponential term describes the decreasing plasma concentration of the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"A single exponential term describes the rise in plasma concentration following oral administration"},{"label":"C","text":"The drug does not penetrate tissues"},{"label":"D","text":"The drug is restricted to the ECF"},{"label":"E","text":"The drug is highly ionised Answers – Pharmacodynamics & Pharmacokinetics 10 August 2004"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8df3abaf8768","stem":"Describe the anatomical organization of the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions of the autonomic nervous system (ANS). Sympathetic division – sympathetic nerves arise from the thoracic and lumbar divisions (thoracolumbar) of the spinal cord. Preganglionic sympathetic fibers originate from neurons in the intermediolateral columns of the spinal cord, are carried over the ventral roots to paravertebral and prevertebral ganglia, and synapse with postganglionic fibers in the sympathetic chain (paravertebral) and abdominal organs (prevertebral). Collateral fibers connect preganglionic fibers with ganglion cells for mass discharge Adrenal medulla – Chromaffin cells, ganglion cells that have not differentiated into neurons, receive innervation from sympathetic preganglionic fibers through the greater splanchnic nerve. Parasympathetic division – parasympathetic nerves arise from cranial nerves (III, VII, IX, and X) and from the sacrum (craniosacral) and synapse with ganglion cells located in the effector organ. Enteric division – consists of an intrinsic intramural collection of myenteric and submucosal plexuses located in the GI tract and modulated by extrinsic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5818fb5620cf","stem":"Describe the sympathetic nervous system response to “fight or flight” situations. General discharge of the sympathetic nerves and adrenal medulla in response to stress is termed the “fight or flight response.” The following are sympathetic responses: • Pupil – dilation • Heart – acceleration • Most arterioles – constriction • Salivary gland – secretion • GI smooth muscle – relaxation • Bronchial smooth muscle – relaxation • Spleen capsule - constriction","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e6263d534fbe","stem":"Describe how single innervation of the arterioles by the sympathetic division can lead to both constriction and dilation of these blood vessels. Arterioles are distensible tubes filled with a liquid under pressure. Vasoconstriction is achieved through","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc41252c8172","stem":"List the three major transmitters of the autonomic nervous system. Acetylcholine, epinephrine, and norepinephrine.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5eb05ea26823","stem":"List the five steps of neurotransmission.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4fbebba04e3e","stem":"Describe the sites in the ANS where acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter. Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter at sympathetic ganglia, parasympathetic ganglia, adrenal medulla, parasympathetic post-ganglionic nerve terminal, and neuromuscular junction of somatic nerves and skeletal muscle.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a2f38dc306d","stem":"Describe the major mechanism by which acetylcholine is inactivated, by which norepinephrine is inactivated. Acetylcholine’s actions are terminated rapidly by acetylcholinesterase, which hydrolyzes acetylcholine into acetate and choline. Choline can then be transported back to be used in acetylcholine synthesis. Norepinephrine’s actions are terminated by (1) reuptake into the terminal, (2) oxidative deamination by monamine oxidase in mitochondria, (3) 0-methylation by catechol-0-methyltransferase, and (4) diffusion away from the synapse.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9fb6b2159b9b","stem":"Identify the sites in the ANS where neurotransmission is mediated by nicotinic or muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. Nicotinic cholinergic receptors are located on: (1) all autonomic sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia (including the adrenal medulla) and (2) the neuromuscular junction of somatic nerves and skeletal muscle. Muscarinic cholinergic receptors are located on: postganglionic parasympathetic neuroeffector cells.","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2f94abe586da","stem":"Describe the sites in the ANS where norepinephrine is used as the neurotransmitter. Norepinephrine is the neurotransmitter for most sympathetic postganglionic neuroeffector junctions. The one exception is innervation of thermoregulatory sweat glands, which uses acetylcholine, although the palms still use norepinephrine (clammy hands when stressed).","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b575802feded","stem":"List the two general types of adrenergic receptors. Alpha (α) and beta (β). Alpha stimulation is usually excitatory (except in the gut). Beta stimulation is usually inhibitory (except in the heart). In the GI tract both relax smooth muscle while in the heart beta simulation increases rate and contractility.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c55bc8e957b","stem":"Describe the origin of epinephrine and its site of release. Origin: The amino acid tyrosine is hydroxylated by tyrosine hydroylase to form dihydroxyphenylalanine (DOPA) and is then decarboxylated by DOPA decarboxylase to form dopamine. Dopamine is hydroxylated in vesicles on the β carbon of the ethylamine side-chain by dopamine β-hydroxylase to form norepinephrine. In certain areas of the brain and the adrenal medulla, norepinephrine is methylated on the amine group by phenylethanolamine-N-methyltransferase to form epinephrine. Site of release: Epinephrine is release from the adrenal medulla into the blood stream, think of it like a neurohormone.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c41142c485a","stem":"Describe the sympathetic/parasympathetic neurotransmitters and their receptors for increasing or decreasing heart rate. |Sympathetic (thoracolumbar) | |↑HR and contractility | |Acetylcholine (preganglionic) |Nicotinic cholinergic receptor |resulting in increased | |Norepinephrine (postganglionic) |Beta-adrenergic (β1) receptor |cardiac output | |Parasympathetic (craniosacral) | |↓HR and contractility | |Acetylcholine (vagal preganglionic) |Nicotinic cholinergic receptor |resulting in ↓CO | |Acetylcholine (postganglionic in heart) |Muscarinic (AV and SA nodes) | |","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cd805645d2b3","stem":"Describe the effects of sympathetic/parasympathetic activation on pupil size and lens accommodation. |Sympathetic (Dark) | |Pupil dilation (mydriasis) | |Acetylcholine (preganglionic) |Nicotinic cholinergic |– dialator pupillae muscle | |Norepinephrine (postganglionic) |Alpha-adrenergic (α1) | | |Parasympathetic (Light) | |Pupil contraction (miosis) | |Acetylcholine (preganglionic) |Nicotinic cholinergic |– constrictor papillae | |Acetylcholine (postganglionic in eye) |Muscarinic (constrictor muscle) |muscle |","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Sympathetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8149519fe5fc","stem":"Describe the mechanisms by which adrenergic agonists and muscarinic antagonists both produce mydriasis. Adrenergic agonists stimulate the alpha-adrenergic receptors (α1), which normally occurs due to norepinephrine released from the postganglionic fibers to the dilator pupillae muscle, resulting in mydriasis. Muscarinic antagonists prevent parasympathetic stimulation of the constrictor papillae muscle by acetylcholine causing it to relax, which results in mydriasis.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d7229b5ee0e3","stem":"Describe what is meant by denervation supersensitivity. When the nervous supply of a muscle or gland is interrupted, the effector organ slowly becomes increasingly sensitive to the neurotransmitter of which it is deprived. This occurs because of upregulation of receptors after one to two weeks.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-45ae609e6fcd","stem":"List the common side effects seen with the use of cholinergic antagonists. Muscarinic receptor antagonists: dry mouth, constipation, decreased sweating, mydriasis (dilation of pupil), urinary retention (especially in elderly males with benign prostatic hypertrophy), tachycardia, decreased lacrimation, precipitation of glaucoma, and decreased respiratory secretions (increased susceptibility to respiratory infections. Nicotinic receptor antagonists (sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglionic blockers): constellation of severe side effects – constipation (blocking enteric ganglia), atony of the bladder, cycloplegia, decreased sweating, postural hypotension, and tachycardia (SA node dominated by parasympathetics).","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71d2d0a99393","stem":"Contrast the therapeutic advantage of pilocarpine to acetylcholine. Pilocarpine exhibits primarily muscarinic receptor agonist actions and it is not degraded by cholinesterases thus it has a 2-3 hour duration. It can be given orally. Acetylcholine however, is a non-selective agonist of both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors and its duration is very brief because it is rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterases. It cannot be given orally.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-edda88efb2f5","stem":"Describe the differences between a direct and indirect acting cholinergic drug. Direct acting agonists bind to the cholinergic receptor and mimic the effects of the natural receptor ligand acetylcholine. Indirect acting agonists inhibit the destruction of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase, thereby increasing the synaptic concentration of acetylcholine which then acts on the cholinergic receptor.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9d7856eeebf2","stem":"Identify the major advantage for using quaternary cholinesterase inhibitors. Quaternary cholinesterase inhibitors, neostigmine and edrophonium, do not significantly cross the blood-brain barrier, and therefore, they are useful for treating peripheral disorders like intestinal and bladder atony and for the management of myasthenia gravis.","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9119775c6e2d","stem":"Describe the drug of choice for reactivating cholinesterase shortly after organophosphate poisoning. The treatment of choice for severe intoxication with centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitors is atropine, given in large doses to combat the central manifestations (confusion, ataxia, convulsions, coma, and respiratory paralysis) and the peripheral signs (bronchial secretions, hypotension, and involuntary twitching leading to paralysis) resulting from overstimulation of muscarinic receptors. Atropine is a muscarinic receptor antagonist, a tertiary amine capable of penetrating into the CNS and blocking all subtypes of muscarinic receptors.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-11f9fa5e3abc","stem":"Describe the major symptoms of a cholinergic crisis. The common signs of “cholinergic crisis” are salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, emesis (“SLUDE” syndrome).","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b4812c18ef37","stem":"Describe the therapeutic uses of muscarinic agonists. Glaucoma – carbachol acts on the muscarinic receptors of the constrictor papillae muscle to produce miosis, thereby opening the canals of Schlemm to facilitate outflow of aqueous humor and decrease intraocular pressure. Diagnosis of achalasia & bronchial airway hyperreactivity – methacholine is used to diagnostically test the response of the lower esophageal sphincter in suspected cases of esophageal achalasia. When inhaled in a NaCl solution it can be used to diagnose bronchial airway hyperreactivity in patients whose asthma is not clinically apparent. Gastrointesinal and urinary bladder atony – bethanechol can be used to increase tone and contractions of the intestine and bladder in patients with postpartum or postsurgical ileus and urinary retention.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-670f30bc8664","stem":"Describe the mechanism by which muscarinic agonists produce vasodilation. Vasodilation is brought about by activation of muscarinic receptors (M3 type) on endothelial cells which promote the release of nitric oxide (endothelium relaxing factor) that diffuses into the vascular smooth muscle and produces relaxation.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ca9922d0e71","stem":"Describe the therapeutic uses of cholinesterase inhibitors. Treat glaucoma – phyostigmine given topically in the eye causes stimulation of the constrictor papillae through activation of muscarinic receptors by acetylcholine. Reverse atropine toxicity – phyostigmine is a tertiary amine that penetrates into the CNS and is therefore capable of reversing the effects of atropine poisoning. Reverse neuromuscular blockers – pyridostigmine reverses the effects of curariform toxicity and other neuromusclular blockers. Treat myasthenia gravis – neostigmine does not cross the blood-brain barrier and therefore works in the periphery to increase muscle strength.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d84d8da0079","stem":"Describe the uses for alpha and beta adrenergic receptor blockers. Objective 31.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05797a395094","stem":"Contrast the effects of dobutamine to isoproterenol. Dobutamine – a beta-1 agonist used primarily to stimulate the heart in cases of cardiogenic shock resulting from an MI. It is also used in cardiac decompensation due to depressed myocardial contractility. Isoproterenol – is a beta-1 and beta-2 agonist used by inhalation as a bronchial dilator, but it also induces tachycardia due to its beta-1 action. This action is useful in cases of transient heart block or cardiac arrest until cardioversion is performed.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-786e23d10735","stem":"Describe how clonidine is thought to exert its antihypertensive effects. Clonidine is a selective alpha-2 receptor agonist acting at presynaptic receptors to inhibit the release of norepinephrine. Its antihypertensive effects are due to activation of alpha-2 receptors in the CNS resulting in decreased sympathetic outflow to the heart and blood vessels.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da4d288e2064","stem":"Contrast the effects of phentolamine and prazosin blockade on the release of norepinephrine, and understand the mechanism responsible for these differences. Phentolamine is a competitive antagonist of both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors. Inhibition of alpha-2 receptors causes excess norepinephrine secretion from cardiac adrenergic neurons with consequent tachycardia. Prozosin is a selective alpha-1 receptor antagonist used in the treatment of hypertension. Blocking alpha-1 receptors inhibits the vasoconstrictor effects of norepinephrine. The lack of alpha-2 effects prevents excess norepinephrine release. Remember that alpha-2 receptors are mainly on presynaptic membranes and alpha-1 receptors are on postsynaptic membranes.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2bb68fc0ae08","stem":"List the common effects of beta and alpha blockers on cardiovascular function. |Agent |Effects |Uses | |Beta Blockers|Bradycardia |Hypertension, Arrhythmias, Angina pectoris, Glaucoma, Migraine | | |Bronchial Constriction |headaches, Stage fright, Prophylaxis after MI | | |Hypotension | | |Alpha |Vasodilation |Hypertension, Raynaud’s disease, Sequelae of frostbite, | |Blockers |Lower BP |Pheochromocytoma, Shock | | |Orthostatic hypotension | | | |Tachycardia | |","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a93722282324","stem":"Describe the mechanism by which beta-blockers (eg. timolol) lower intraocular pressure. Timolol is a beta-1 and beta-2 blocker used in the eye for the management of glaucoma. The thought is that beta receptors promote formation of aqueous humor, so by blocking them, the rate of aqueous production and thus intraocular pressure is ↓.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59c390d71b0f","stem":"Describe therapeutic uses for alpha-adrenergic receptor blockers. Raynaud’s disease – phentolamine prevents vasospasm related to this disease. Pheochromocytoma – phentolamine prevents the HTN and tachycardia. Erectile dysfunction – phentolamine can be combined with papaverine and a prostaglandin for corpus cavernosum injections. Hypertension – prazosin interferes with the vasoconstrictor effects of norepinephrine thus allowing vasodilation and decreasing TPR. Urinary retention due to benign postatic hyperplasia – prazosin, terazosin, and doxazosin relax bladder neck and prostate.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30f7fa8e35f1","stem":"Describe dopamine’s mechanism of action. Dopamine exerts agonist effects on adrenergic as well as on dopaminergic receptors. Dopamine D1 receptors are especially prominent in the renal and mesenteric vascular beds and their activation causes dilation of the blood vessels. Dopamine D2 autoreceptors are important in the modulation of neurotransmitter release. In low doses its preferential action is to stimulate dopamine receptors producing relaxation of vascular smooth muscle in the renal blood vessels, thereby improving renal flow which is compromised in shock. Moderate doses stimulate beta-1 receptors in the heart to increase CO. higher doses recruit the actions of alpha-1 adrenergic receptors to produce vasoconstriction, increased BP, and less vasoconstriction in renal vessels than elsewhere.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ec81a1f414c","stem":"Describe the mechanism of action of indirectly acting sympathomimetics. The main action of these compounds is not through the stimulation of adrenergic receptors, but rather through the release of norepinephrine or blockade of its inactivation by preventing its reuptake. Ephedrine causes the release of norepinephrine in the CNS (stimulation) and specifically on beta-2 receptors (bronchodilation and vasodilation). Pseudoephedrine has less CNS and beta-2 effects but is used because of its vasoconstrictive properties. Amphetamine causes norepinephrine release resulting in stimulation, increased BP and heart rate. Cocaine blocks norepinephrine reuptake causing euphoria, but it may also be used in surgery for its vasoconstrictive and anesthetic properties.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fff3174a6e38","stem":"The following are risk factors for uterine cervical cancer except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Having multiple sexual partners"},{"label":"B","text":"Young age at first intercourse"},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent infection with high oncogenic HPV virusses (e.g 16 or 18)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nicotine use"},{"label":"D","text":"Cervicitis"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-68f317ed3d90","stem":"esophagus. A biopsy reveals glands with goblet cells. What is in this context the explanation for the presence of these cells?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-02302d1d52d9","stem":"underlying mechanism of atrophy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8af66efaaa69","stem":"considered as the etiology of his disease?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d77650add159","stem":"Which factor(s) is (are) the most important inducer(s) of vasodilatation","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f14ba12d2ce7","stem":"Which of the following items occurs first in acute inflammation?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General Pathology","topicOriginal":"General Pathology","subtopic":"Cell Injury and Inflammation","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0d44334f40dc","stem":"Aspirin is often used as for its anti-inflammatory effects. Which of the following processes is influenced by aspirin?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General Pathology","topicOriginal":"General Pathology","subtopic":"Cell Injury and Inflammation","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c0d8cab07721","stem":"Which of the following molecules plays a role in chronic inflammation and not in acute inflammation?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General Pathology","topicOriginal":"General Pathology","subtopic":"Cell Injury and Inflammation","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-14fbc14e6a7d","stem":"Which statement is correct about a keloid?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"13666f26fd8330b896f40304984d43f68fe93a80"},{"id":"q-f4b92776814f","stem":"Which statement is correct for Trisomy 21?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9e7f4878dd77","stem":"Which of the following features is characteristic for Marfan syndrome?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f8217a3e1df3","stem":"Which of the following features makes the diagnosis Turner syndrome unlikely?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aa8796768131","stem":"The benign tumor of the breast is called?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42b6e793a58c","stem":"to indicate that the neoplasm is malignant?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-09a525305528","stem":"which has no microcalcifications, and reveals no lesions of the opposite breast. Which of the following is the most likely","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c8980847e22c","stem":"week. On physical examination her temperature is 38.2°C. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88046f1c22c4","stem":"Which statement is correctabout a keloid?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"13666f26fd8330b896f40304984d43f68fe93a80"},{"id":"q-e7002215c36b","stem":".Malignant melanomas are malignant proliferation of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melasma"},{"label":"B","text":"Acanthosis nigricans"},{"label":"C","text":"Seborrhea keratosis D . Melanocytes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-22d92546589e","stem":".Macules Refers to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Circumscribed lesion of 5>mm in diameter characterized by flatness and usually discolored (often red)."},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated dome-shaped or flat-topped lesion <5mm across. C . Circumscribed lesion of <5mm in diameter characterized by flatness and usually discolored (often red). D. Discrete, pus-filled, raised lesion"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ad34388d9252","stem":".. Excessive accumulation of iron deposited in liver, pancreas and heart is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"optio