iting blood is also known as ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haematemesis"},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoptysis"},{"label":"C","text":"Haematuria"},{"label":"D","text":"Haemulceration Answer A"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e9e33b765257ce26d9e4f05a864057b19bc9e037"},{"id":"q-8c75821d5ddc","stem":"Difficulty in swallowing is called?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aphasia"},{"label":"B","text":"Dysphagia"},{"label":"C","text":"Dysphasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Dyspnoea"},{"label":"E","text":"Dysplasia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"30f1f79985db38e75413f4b622785de744631318"},{"id":"q-9096b9ecc7dc","stem":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding Barret's Oesophagus?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macroscopic findings include distal extension of the squamo-columnar junction"},{"label":"B","text":"Risk factors include male gender, caucasian ethnicity and obesity"},{"label":"C","text":"Histological findings show squamous epithelia being replaced by columnar"},{"label":"D","text":"Patients are at a significantly increased risk of developing adenocarcinoma"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05ac1498b303","stem":"Which of the following cytologic features of malignant cells is most important in distinguishing benign from malignant lesions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear enlargement."},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear hyperchromasia."},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleolar enlargement."},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased cohesion."},{"label":"E","text":"Mitotic activity"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0fc8ebd1aa72","stem":"Which of the following terms refers to soft deposits of intra-arterial fat and fibrin in the vessel walls that hardens over time?a","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arteriosclerosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Atherosclerosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Atherosclerotic disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombo-embolism"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fddf820eedbd","stem":"A diabetic patient comes to the doctor complaining about sever pain in his legs brought on by ambulation. The nurse suspects which of the following conditions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atherosclerosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral Artery Disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Arteriosclerosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Angina pectoris"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3ad915e8da93","stem":"Which of the following is the most common reason for myocardial ischemia?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronary Spasms"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypotension"},{"label":"C","text":"Atherosclerotic plaques"},{"label":"D","text":"Male gender and old age"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-979367218746","stem":"44-year-old woman notes a lump in her left breast while taking a shower. Her physician notes a 3 cm firm, irregular, non- movable mass located in the upper outer quadrant of her left breast on physical examination. A fine needle aspiration of this mass is performed. Cells obtained from the mass are examined cytologically and are consistent with infiltrating ductal carcinoma. The mass is removed with lumpectomy along with an axillary lymph node dissection. Which of the following findings will best predict a better prognosis for this patient? A The tumor cells are strongly estrogen receptor positive B No metastases are found in the sampled lymph nodes C Flow cytometric analysis demonstrates aneuploidy and a high S-phase D She has one relative who had a similar type of breast cancer E The tumor has a high grade","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58611abf031d","stem":"Accumulation of fluid in the pericardial cavity is referred to as which of the following terms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericarditis"},{"label":"B","text":"Pericardial Effusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Tamponade"},{"label":"D","text":"pleural effusion"},{"label":"E","text":"ascites"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a6521474451e","stem":"Which of the following terms refers to to rapid accumulation of fluid in the pericardial cavity, causing cardiac compression","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleural effusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Pericardial Effusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Tamponede"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62c814c4ea9b","stem":"The image below shows aorta with a lesion. This is termed as?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"aortic aneurysm"},{"label":"B","text":"aortic dissection"},{"label":"C","text":"embolus"},{"label":"D","text":"hematoma"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1cc0c3c06745","stem":"A 45-year-old man was rushed to the hospital following the sudden onset of an episode of crushing substernal chest pain. He receives advanced life support measures. An EKG shows changes consistent with a large transmural anterolateral area of infarction involving wall of the left ventricle. He develops cardiogenic shock. Which of the following microscopic findings is most likely to be present in this area 4 days following the onset of his chest pain? A [pic] Fibroblasts and collagen deposition B [pic] Capillary proliferation and macrophages C [pic] Myofiber necrosis with neutrophils D [pic] Granulomatous inflammation E [pic] Perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General Pathology","topicOriginal":"General Pathology","subtopic":"Cell Injury and Inflammation","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-424a7ca7056e","stem":"A 41-year-old man has a history of drinking 1 to 2 liters of whisky per day for the past 20 years. He has had numerous episodes of nausea and vomiting in the past 5 years. He now experiences a bout of prolonged vomiting, followed by massive hematemesis. On physical examination his vital signs are: T 36.9°C, P 110/min, RR 26/min, and BP 80/40 mm Hg lying down. His heart has a regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs and his lungs are clear to auscultation. There is no abdominal tenderness or distension and bowel sounds are present. His stool is negative for occult blood. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? A [pic] Hiatal hernia B [pic] Esophageal laceration C [pic] Esophageal pulsion diverticulum D [pic] Barrett esophagus E [pic] Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma F [pic] Esophageal stricture","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-903c5b87cbe3","stem":"A 58-year-old man has had increasing difficulty swallowing for the past 6 months and has lost 5 kg. No abnormal physical examination findings are noted. Upper GI endoscopy reveals a nearly circumferential mass with overlying ulceration in the mid esophageal region. Biopsy of the mass reveals pink polygonal cells with marked hyperchromatism and pleomorphism. Which of the following is the most likely risk factor for development of his disease? A [pic] Iron deficiency B [pic] Helicobacter pylori infection C [pic] Chronic alcohol abuse D [pic] High fruit diet E [pic] Zenker diverticulum","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6ce2f4408201","stem":"A 23-year-old woman has had worsening malaise along with a malar skin rash persisting for 3 weeks. On physical examination, she has an audible friction rub on auscultation of the chest, along with a faint systolic murmur. An echocardiogram reveals small vegetations on the mitral valve and adjacent ventricular endocardium. Laboratory studies show a positive serologic test for anti- Smith antibody, with a titer of 1:2048. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? A [pic] Polyarteritis nodosa B [pic] Scleroderma, diffuse C [pic] Systemic lupus erythematosus D [pic] ANCA-associated granulomatous vasculitis E [pic] Adenocarcinoma of the pancreas","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3c8772d5da43","stem":"A 27-year-old G2 P1 woman has a screening ultrasound performed at 18 weeks gestation. The fetus is appropriate in size for 18 weeks. The fetal kidneys, liver, head, and extremities appear normal. However, the fetus has a heart with a membranous ventricular septal defect, overriding aorta, and marked pulmonic atresia. If the baby were to be liveborn, which of the following characteristics on physical examination would most likely result from these cardiac defects? A [pic] Systemic hypertension B [pic] Weak lower extremity pulses C [pic] Clubbing of digits D [pic] Telangiectasias E [pic] Cyanosis","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a110f5508081","stem":"A 44-year-old woman dies as a consequence of a 'stroke'. At autopsy, she is found to have a large right basal ganglia hemorrhage. She has an enlarged 550 gm heart with predominantly left ventricular hypertrophy. Her kidneys are small, about 80 gm each, with cortical scarring, and microscopically they demonstrate small renal arterioles that have luminal narrowing from concentric intimal thickening. Which of the following is the most likely condition associated with her findings? A [pic] Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease B [pic] Diabetes mellitus, type II C [pic] Hypercholesterolemia D [pic] Hypertensive emergency E [pic] Monckeberg's sclerosis","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89aec1b4853c","stem":"Dried exudate on the surface of the skin.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plaque"},{"label":"B","text":"Crust"},{"label":