ost often associated with Birth Asphyxia and signs of hypoxic Ischemic Encephalopathy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"below 5 at 1 minute"},{"label":"B","text":"5 and below at 5 minutes"},{"label":"C","text":"any score below 10 at 10 minutes"},{"label":"D","text":"below 8 at 5 minutes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8e15110a3075d4b7d24c4358ef95f39439186b1f"},{"id":"q-774924cfdc81","stem":"Severe dehydration is best characterized by the following signs age according to WHO classification?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"lethargy, reduced skin turgor, reduced urine output"},{"label":"B","text":"irritability, reduced skin turgor"},{"label":"C","text":"thirst, eager to drink, dry mouth"},{"label":"D","text":"inability to pass urine, edema of the feet Which of the following findings would be most significant to make the diagnosis of severe pneumonia in a child of 2 years of"},{"label":"A","text":"CXR showing bilateral infiltrates"},{"label":"B","text":"Tachypnea, subcostal indrawings, ill looking"},{"label":"C","text":"Tachypnea, Leucocytosis in Full Blood picture"},{"label":"D","text":"Cyanosis and clubbing"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a8ab344d7e8","stem":"Which is the most common group of bacteria causing bacterial Meningitis in a child above 6 months?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmonella, Streptococcus Group A, Staphylococcus"},{"label":"B","text":"E. Coli, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae, Meningococcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Beta haemolytic Streptococcus Group B, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. Coli"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-7a4ed69ad0e7","stem":"Which is the most common group of bacteria causing bacterial Meningitis in a neonate of 2 weeks old?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Bacteriology","topicOriginal":"Bacteriology","subtopic":"Bacterial Pathogens","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmonella, Streptococcus Group A, Staphylococcus"},{"label":"B","text":"E. Coli, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae, Meningococcus"},{"label":"D","text":"Beta haemolytic Streptococcus Group B, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. Coli"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Bacteriology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-15278aac919e","stem":"A “complex partial seizure” is defined as a seizure presenting with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"focal motor or sensory signs without impairment of consciousness"},{"label":"B","text":"twitching of either both arms or both legs, but never the whole body"},{"label":"C","text":"aura, followed by twitching of one side of the body without loss of consciousness"},{"label":"D","text":"focal motor, sensory or autonomic signs associated with impairment of consciousness"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-649625b29a90","stem":"A neonate was admitted with history of inability to suck, fever and vomiting. Blood slide for malaria was positive. How will you treat the neonate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quinine 10mg/kg 8 - 12 hourly for seven days"},{"label":"B","text":"Quinine 10mg/kg 8 hourly for three days"},{"label":"C","text":"Quinine 7mg/kg 12hourly for seven days"},{"label":"D","text":"Quinine 10mg/kg + antibiotics"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a59f0c752635","stem":"Neck stiffness can be a sign of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"bacterial meningitis"},{"label":"B","text":"viral meningitis"},{"label":"C","text":"TB meningitis"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-171e8b811697","stem":"Facial nerve palsy affecting the lower motor neuron","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"leads to impairment of eye closure and inability to close the mouth properly on the same side of the face"},{"label":"B","text":"presents only with inability to close the eye on the same side of the face"},{"label":"C","text":"leads to a mask like appearance of the whole face without facial expression"},{"label":"D","text":"leads to inability to close the mouth and smile on the opposite side of the face"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fca6ee3aa02f","stem":"A 3 year old child is brought to you because of easy fatigability and cough. You observe finger clubbing and peripheral and central cyanosis. All of the following are differential diagnosis, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"cyanotic congenital heart disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchiectasis"},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic renal failure"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a388d59eb263","stem":"The following physical signs may be present in pericardial effusion","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsatile liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Pulsus paradoxus"},{"label":"C","text":"a left parasternal heave"},{"label":"D","text":"a loud first sound at the apex"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bb8aa0d04071e9097cb70e1c4ddf10fbf2a050f6"},{"id":"q-53bd6d0e7610","stem":"The following statement about Oral rehydration solution is correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should contain Glucose, Sodium chloride, Potassium, Bicarbonate"},{"label":"B","text":"It should contain Glucose, Sodium Chloride, and Magnesium"},{"label":"C","text":"Must be Glucose free, because glucose stimulates the diarrhea"},{"label":"D","text":"Must be Potassium free, as many dehydrated patients are at risk to go into renal failure"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7caf3eeb0393","stem":"How can we define Oliguria in a child?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"When a child passes urine only once per day"},{"label":"B","text":"When a child passes less than 1ml/kg/hour"},{"label":"C","text":"When a child passes less than 400 mls/ day"},{"label":"D","text":"When a child passes no urine at all."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b11ecb90d81d","stem":"In a developing country HIV infection mother to child vertical transmission is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"> 50%"},{"label":"B","text":">40%"},{"label":"C","text":"30-40%"},{"label":"D","text":"10-20%"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-15305176cabe","stem":"HIV in children is different from adults because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"children have lower CD4 cells as compared to adults"},{"label":"B","text":"children responds better to ART as compared to adults"},{"label":"C","text":"most of the children progresses faster as compared to adults"},{"label":"D","text":"children presents with lesser opportunistic infections as compared to adults"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b022b7d3ade","stem":"HIV exposed baby born to a mother who is on 1st line ART regime for 3 years should receive the following as prophylaxis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"single dose niverapine for prophylaxis at birth"},{"label":"B","text":"niverapine single dose and AZT for 7 days"},{"label":"C","text":"Trimune baby for 1 month"},{"label":"D","text":"single dose niverapine and AZT for 1 month"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-18c84f9a8818","stem":"In which valvular heart conditions we do not hear a systolic murmur?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"mitral stenosis"},{"label":"B","text":"pulmonary stenosis"},{"label":"C","text":"mitral incompetence"},{"label":"D","text":"ventricular septal defect (VSD)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4e55a8449017","stem":"The following physical signs may be present in pericardial effusion","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsatile liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Pulsus paradoxus"},{"label":"C","text":"A left parasternal heave"},{"label":"D","text":"A loud first sound in the apex"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bb8aa0d04071e9097cb70e1c4ddf10fbf2a050f6"},{"id":"q-7ed5113ab448","stem":". What are the 3 initial steps in resuscitation in a child with cardiorespiratory arrest?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV fluids, O2 and breathing security"},{"label":"B","text":"Secure airway, assisted breathing, cardiac massage"},{"label":"C","text":"thermal management, clearing the airway, tactile stimulation"},{"label":"D","text":"Oxygen by mask, cardiac massage, IV. fluids"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"70cbe5054631b5ca311a2fc361d4d06dc3111716"},{"id":"q-88196e9fe534","stem":". Regarding infant feeding and HIV","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"One of the feeding options is exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months"},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed feeding is the best option if the mother is working"},{"label":"C","text":"Out of 20 breastfeeding women 5 are likely to be infected"},{"label":"D","text":"In exclusive breastfeeding the mother can give some water"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"02393adc053ea34f10f4e42df46ec5700d92dbb1"},{"id":"q-ac0eb63e4da3","stem":". What are the contraindications for BCG vaccination?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Children with active HIV infection"},{"label":"B","text":"Children who are born prematurely"},{"label":"C","text":"Children with history of TB contact"},{"label":"D","text":"No contraindication to a child of below 5 years of age"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9d079bf30ab8e43cf3605ebbb15e34a0789f0b36"},{"id":"q-1be0e027943c","stem":"HIV in children, the following is a specific feature for children","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"tuberculosis"},{"label":"B","text":"lymphadenopathies"},{"label":"C","text":"lymphoid interstitial pneumonia (LIP)"},{"label":"D","text":"wasting syndrome"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6865fa3be4ba","stem":". Baby Mari is a 4 months old. He has a CD4 count of 900cell body weight 6kg and DNA- PCR positive His doctor has decided to start him on ARVs. Which one of the factors below helped the doctor to make this decision to start ARVs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". He has lost body weight"},{"label":"B","text":". He is HIV positive"},{"label":"C","text":". The doctor made wrong decision since CD4 count is still high"},{"label":"D","text":". His age"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06496ca5c512","stem":"Which of the following is the most acceptable core intervention for the prevention of mother to child transmission?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV testing of the mother after delivery"},{"label":"B","text":"Discouraging women with HIV from having children"},{"label":"C","text":"ARV treatment or prophylaxis to reduce maternal viral load"},{"label":"D","text":"Discourage women with HIV from breastfeeding"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54a57a85d3b9","stem":"Juma is a 3 year old child who has just been referred from Mawenzi hospital for HIV care to your clinic. The doctor has decided to put him on ARVs. What can be used to monitor his treatment success?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". His CD4 percentage"},{"label":"B","text":". His total lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":". His CD4 absolute count"},{"label":"D","text":". His current WHO stage(16)."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a1c6961e5af8","stem":"A 8 year old girl presents with a history of recurrent pneumonia on physical examination. Wheezing and crackles are heard and finger clubbing is evident. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asthma"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchiectasis"},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary sequestration"},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngomalacia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4af1c2362c86","stem":"A patient with newly diagnosed acute lymphocytic leukemia who presents with a Neutrophil count of less than 1000 WBC and temperature of 40 degree Celsius should be managed by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematological Malignancies","topicOriginal":"Hematological Malignancies","subtopic":"Leukemia and Lymphoma","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prompt institution of chemotherapy because the fever is most likely due to the leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Tepid sponging and wait for fever to come down"},{"label":"C","text":"Promptly obtaining cultures and start broad spectrum antibiotics parentally"},{"label":"D","text":"Vigorous antipyretic therapy with aspirin to bring down the temperature"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematological Malignancies","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2b6aab76ac9e","stem":".All of the following are typical manifestations of an insulin reaction (hypoglycaemia) in insulin dependant diabetes EXCEPT","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"Diabet","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sweating"},{"label":"B","text":"Severe dehydration"},{"label":"C","text":"Lethargy"},{"label":"D","text":"Bizarre behaviour"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-34da051be556","stem":"The normal range for a newborn heart rate is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"60-80"},{"label":"B","text":"80-100"},{"label":"C","text":"110-150"},{"label":"D","text":"140-200"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b8826353d847","stem":". One of the following is commonly observed in the premature infants","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"abundance of lanugo"},{"label":"B","text":"Dry, peeling skin"},{"label":"C","text":"Long nails"},{"label":"D","text":"palmar creases on the whole foot"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24ccbd101168","stem":". Which of the following are complications of cold stress (hypothermia)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depletion of energy stores"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased oxygen consumption"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["to